---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SnapshotForm from '../../components/SnapshotForm.astro';
import { site } from '../../config/site';
import ServiceFAQ from '../../components/ServiceFAQ.astro';
import { servicePageSchema, snapshotOffers, type Faq } from '../../lib/schema';
const description = 'A free PostgreSQL health check: run one read-only script on RDS, Aurora or self-managed PostgreSQL. If it fits, get 3 written performance findings within 2 business days.';
const faq: Faq[] = [
  { q: 'Is the health check script safe to run on production?', a: 'It sets the session to read-only, gives each statement a 10-second timeout and a 1-second lock timeout, and reads only PostgreSQL statistics and catalogs. It creates nothing and changes no persistent setting.' },
  { q: 'Does the script send data anywhere?', a: 'No. It writes a text file on your machine. You read it, remove anything you do not want to share, and upload it yourself through the form.' },
  { q: 'Can I leave out query text and table structure?', a: 'Yes. Add -v show_query_text=off to omit query text and -v show_schema=off to omit the column listing. The findings are less specific without query text.' },
  { q: 'Why only a few snapshots a month?', a: `Each one is read and written up by hand, by one person. I accept up to ${site.snapshotMonthlyCap} a month and tell you within 1 business day if the month is full or the output is not usable.` },
  { q: 'What happens after I get the findings?', a: 'Nothing, unless you ask. If a finding needs a deeper answer, I say whether the paid slowdown audit fits and what it would cover.' }
];
const scriptPath = '/snapshot/walstack-snapshot.sql';
const command = 'psql -X -h <host> -p 5432 -U <user> -d <database> ' + '\\' + '\n     -f walstack-snapshot.sql -o walstack-snapshot-1.txt';
---
<BaseLayout title="Free PostgreSQL Health Check & Performance Snapshot | WalStack" description={description} schema={servicePageSchema({ name: 'Free PostgreSQL health check and performance snapshot', path: '/snapshot', description, breadcrumb: 'Free PostgreSQL health check', faq, offers: snapshotOffers })}>
  <header class="container hero"><h1>Free PostgreSQL health check and performance snapshot</h1>
    <p class="lede">Run one read-only script against your database and send me the output. I'll email 3 findings covering what stands out, why it matters and what I'd check next.</p>
    <p>Free, no call needed. I review up to {site.snapshotMonthlyCap} snapshots a month from companies running PostgreSQL in production. I first check that the output is usable and the request fits. If it doesn't, or the month is full, I'll tell you within 1 business day. Accepted snapshots get 3 written findings within 2 business days of acceptance.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="#download">Get the script</a><a class="button" href={site.bookingUrl} data-track="book_call_click">{site.callLabel}</a></div>
  </header>
  <section><div class="container prose snapshot">
    <h2 id="download">1. Download the script</h2>
    <a class="button primary" href={scriptPath} download="walstack-snapshot.sql" data-track="script_download">Download walstack-snapshot.sql</a>
    <h2>2. Run it during a busy period</h2>
    <p>Use PostgreSQL 13 or newer on RDS, Aurora or a self-managed server. Use an account with <code>pg_monitor</code>, PostgreSQL's built-in monitoring role. On RDS or Aurora, an administrator can grant it with <code>GRANT pg_monitor TO &lt;user&gt;;</code>.</p>
    <p>Open a terminal in the script's folder. Replace the host, user and database names with your own.</p>
    <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="psql command"><code id="run-command">{command}</code></pre>
    <button type="button" class="button" id="copy-command" hidden>Copy command</button><span id="copy-status" role="status"></span>
    <p>One run is fine. If you can, run it again about an hour later. Change the output filename to <code>walstack-snapshot-2.txt</code>.</p>
    <p>Comparing two runs shows what's growing now. A single run gives totals since statistics were last reset.</p>
    <p>The script usually takes a few seconds. Each statement has a 10-second timeout. If it waits on a lock for one second, the script continues to the next statement.</p>
    <h2>3. Check what you're sharing</h2>
    <h3>What it reads</h3><ul>
      <li>PostgreSQL's own statistics and catalog, including settings, expensive queries, table and index activity, and index definitions.</li>
      <li>Foreign keys that may need a supporting index, blocked sessions, connections, cleanup activity and replication health.</li>
      <li>Column names and types for the 10 largest tables. Add <code>-v show_schema=off</code> to the command to omit the column listing. Index and foreign key definitions can still contain column names.</li></ul>
    <h3>What it leaves alone</h3><ul>
      <li>It doesn't read rows from your application tables, write data, change persistent settings or create objects.</li>
      <li>It doesn't run your queries or ask PostgreSQL to explain their execution plans.</li>
      <li>It doesn't record host names or IP addresses. It does include database, table, index and role names.</li>
      <li>PostgreSQL usually replaces query values with markers like <code>$1</code> and <code>$2</code>. Some statements, such as <code>ALTER ROLE … PASSWORD</code>, keep their literal values. The script replaces any query text that mentions a password, secret, token or API key with a placeholder. Sensitive text can still appear, so add <code>-v show_query_text=off</code> to omit query text entirely. Findings are less specific without it.</li></ul>
    <p>Read every file before sending. Delete anything you don't want to share, including sensitive names or details in screenshots.</p>
    <h2 id="send">4. Send your snapshot</h2><SnapshotForm />
    <h2>5. What happens to your data?</h2>
    <p>I use your output only to write your findings. Formspree handles the upload. I keep the files private and delete them after {site.retentionDays} days unless you hire me. <a href="/privacy">Read my privacy policy</a>.</p>
    <ServiceFAQ items={faq} />
  </div></section>
  <script>
    const copy = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('#copy-command');
    if (copy && navigator.clipboard) {
      copy.hidden = false;
      copy.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        const status = document.querySelector('#copy-status')!;
        try {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(document.querySelector('#run-command')!.textContent || '');
          status.textContent = ' Command copied.';
        } catch { status.textContent = ' Select and copy the command above.'; }
      });
    }
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
